Overview

This document records the architectural decisions adopted for the Ecommerce Data Warehouse on AWS.

Each decision describes its context, the selected approach, and its technical consequences.

The purpose is to maintain architectural consistency throughout the implementation and provide a clear rationale for the technologies and design patterns used in the project.

ADR-001 — Amazon Redshift Serverless as the Data Warehouse

Status: Accepted

Context

The project requires a centralized analytical warehouse capable of supporting ecommerce reporting and analytical queries.

The infrastructure should minimize the operational overhead associated with managing and maintaining provisioned warehouse clusters.

Decision

Use Amazon Redshift Serverless as the analytical data warehouse.

Consequences

AWS manages the underlying warehouse compute infrastructure.

Capacity configuration must reflect workload requirements.

Storage and compute usage must be monitored to manage costs.

The warehouse remains responsible for analytical storage and SQL query execution.

ADR-002 — Star Schema for Dimensional Modeling

Status: Accepted

Context

The platform must support analytical queries across ecommerce orders, customers, products, payment methods, and shipment status.

A dimensional model provides a structure that is easier to understand and query for reporting and business intelligence.

Decision

Adopt a star schema with fact_order_items as the central fact table and five associated dimensions:

dim_customers

dim_products

dim_date

dim_payment_methods

dim_shipment_status

The fact table grain is one row per order item.

Consequences

Analytical measures are stored in the fact table.

Descriptive attributes are maintained in dimension tables.

Surrogate keys establish warehouse relationships.

The model supports consistent analytical queries across business dimensions.

ADR-003 — SCD Type 2 for Customer and Product Dimensions

Status: Accepted

Context

Customer and product attributes can change over time.

Overwriting existing dimension records would remove historical information and limit the ability to analyze business events using the attributes that were valid when those events occurred.

Decision

Implement Slowly Changing Dimension Type 2 for dim_customers and dim_products.

Consequences

Dimension records use surrogate keys.

Historical versions are preserved instead of overwritten.

valid_from and valid_to define each version's validity period.

is_current identifies the active version.

Fact records must reference the appropriate historical dimension version.

ADR-004 — AWS Glue for Data Cataloging and Integration

Status: Accepted

Context

Ecommerce source datasets are stored in Amazon S3 and require metadata management and data integration capabilities before being consumed by the analytical warehouse.

Decision

Use AWS Glue for data cataloging and integration workflows.

Consequences

Glue Data Catalog maintains metadata about source datasets.

Glue supports dataset discovery and schema management.

Glue integration workflows prepare data for downstream processing.

The integration layer remains separate from analytical transformations managed by dbt Core.

ADR-005 — Amazon S3 as the Data Lake Storage Layer

Status: Accepted

Context

The architecture requires a storage layer that separates original source datasets from intermediate data prepared for warehouse loading.

Preserving source data also provides a basis for reprocessing and auditing.

Decision

Use Amazon S3 as the data lake storage layer, with two logical zones:

Raw: original source datasets.

Staging: intermediate datasets prepared for warehouse loading.

Consequences

Source data and prepared data remain logically separated.

Data preparation does not overwrite the original source datasets.

S3 provides the storage foundation for AWS Glue integration workflows.

Retention, lifecycle, encryption, and access policies must be defined as part of infrastructure configuration.

ADR-006 — dbt Core for SQL Transformations

Status: Accepted

Context

The warehouse requires a structured transformation layer with version-controlled SQL logic, reusable models, data quality tests, and generated documentation.

Decision

Use dbt Core to manage transformations within Amazon Redshift.

Consequences

SQL transformations are maintained as project source code.

Models are organized into staging, intermediate, and marts layers.

Data quality rules are implemented through dbt tests.

dbt documentation provides model descriptions and lineage information.

Transformation logic remains separate from AWS infrastructure provisioning.

ADR-007 — AWS Step Functions for Workflow Orchestration

Status: Accepted

Context

The data workflow contains dependent processing stages, including data integration, warehouse loading, and analytical transformations.

These stages require coordination to establish execution order and manage workflow failures.

Decision

Use AWS Step Functions as the workflow orchestration service.

Consequences

Workflow execution is defined through state machines.

Processing stages can be coordinated according to their dependencies.

Retry and failure-handling behavior can be defined at the workflow level.

The orchestration layer remains separate from the transformation logic implemented in dbt Core.

Decision Governance

These decisions establish the architectural baseline for the project.

Any future architectural change should document:

The technical or business requirement motivating the change.

The alternatives considered.

The selected approach and its rationale.

The expected impact on infrastructure, data processing, operations, and cost.

The README, architecture documentation, data model, Terraform configuration, and dbt project should remain consistent with the accepted decisions.
